Date: Sat, 2 Oct 93 14:16:13 -0400
From: Jeff Fabijanic <jeff@PROFESSOR-ED.ELF.COM>
Subject: HEALTH: Penicillin

Eli uses writes of his room-mate being prescribed the "relatively benign" drug  
penicillin for a viral infection.

1) Penicillin is ineffective against viral infection.

2) The over-enthusiastic use of anti-biotics by the medical community is  
producing new resistant strains of all sorts of nasty bugs. It's having a  
pronounced effect on all forms of infectious disease caused by cocci.

3) Far from being "benign", penicillin (and the the other 'cillans', such as  
ampicillin), can cause extreme allergic reactions in a non-trivial percentage  
of people. This reaction can actually be potentiated by overuse of these drugs.  
My fiancee, for instance, was treated with penicillin for mononucleosis as a  
teen. She developed a slight allergic reaction after prolonged use. When she  
was in her sophomore year at MIT, she became ill. She went to the infirmary,  
and they treated her with ampicillin (even though she told them she thought she  
might be allergic). She went into shock and nearly died.

She now has a medic-alert necklace. I make sure she wears it whenever she goes  
out (takes some doing in the winter, as that sucker gets COLD!). 


Eli, it's just my opinion, but I think your roomate should get a new doctor.
(You can bet Kelly did, pronto).

 - Jeff

