Date: Sat, 2 Oct 1993 14:33:36 -0400 (EDT)
From: Edward J OConnell <ejo@world.std.com>
Subject: HEALTH: Penicillin

The widespread overuse of Penicillin has some interesting ramifications
for such AC ideas such as the elimination of the FDA, the MD cartel, etc.

Specifically, hypochondriacs would become breeding grounds for resistant
strains. A strange example wherein something I do to my body impacts the
community. While I find this argument stupid and trivial when it comes to
recreational drugs, the resistant strain thing gives me pause.

I'm hypersensitive to certain infections--I can feel strep infections very
clearly, for example. It is annoying to have to pay someone at a walk in
clinic 100 dollars to confirm my suspicion and give me a note for the
pharmicist. (The strep test is cool, by the way, its instant now--no waiting.)

What would be nice, I guess, would be a strep test I could buy in the drug
store, and show to my pharmicist, thus eliminating the doctor from the
loop for this trivial ailment.(Not trivial if untreated though--there are
long term consequences for ignoring strep) Probably some sort of proof of
non-allergic status, too, to protect the Pharm. from litigation. One can
imagine diagnostic tools coming within our reach (pregnancy tests, etc)
and self treatment based on the results of these tests. (Abortion pills,
antibiotics) Home expert health systems. Shrink the health care profession
with more DIY.

Of course, this runs counter to the philosophy of the AMA, in which they
are the only people qualified to do anything to a human body.

In response to an old thread about depression, I thought I'd belatedly add
my voice to those that suggested exercise as a treatment. I was treated
for Anxiety disorder for over a year with an antidepressant, and while
grateful at the time for the drug, I wish my Doctor had suggested
exercise. 

When the anxiety returned, while watching my father-in-law slowly waste
away from a mystery disease (a Christian scientist putting his faith to
the test. So brave. So stupid. He's dead and gone now.) I decided to give
exercise a try rather than go back on medication. It worked. I've kept it
up, more or less, since then. 

I sincerely hope my timetables for the singularity are wrong, and I never
have to be frozen.

