Date: 	Sat, 2 Oct 1993 13:02:02 -0700
From: John K Clark <johnkc@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: Everett interpretation of QM

  I know of no theory in science that has been more successful,
over a wider range of conditions than Quantum Mechanics but it
has always suffered from philosophical problems. Is that a wave
or a particle? In what direction is that photon polarized? The
standard Copenhagen interpretation, believed by most Physicists,
is  that nothing is anything until a measurement is made and the
wave function collapses . If you decide to use a photographic
plate, an electron is a particle. If you decide to use an
interferometer, an electron is a wave. This works fine except
its a bit vague about what a measurement is and who is taking
the measurement. Are Human Beings the only ones able to make an
observation and change a potential reality into a actual one.
What about a chimp or a dog or a bug? In 1957 Hugh Everett
solved the measurement problem but at a very high price , too
high most thought. He said that when any particle undergoes the
smallest detectable change ( a quantum jump ) the entire
universe duplicates itself and splits in two but we have no way
of communicating with the other universe. If true this would
mean an infinite number of universes. Many would differ from
this one in only one tiny detail but some would be very
different. Even EXTREMELY unlikely events would happen
someplace. In one I was just elected The Pope in another I  just
graduated from Ringling Brothers Clown Collage and in one all
the molecules of air diffused by chance to the other side of the
room as I type this and I suffocate.

  When I first heard of this I was not very impressed because it
seemed like untestable Meta- physical speculation but then in
1986 David Deutsch proposed a method of testing it ( The Ghost
In The Atom, Cambridge University Press. page 83-105). This at
least brought it to the realm of Science but unfortunately it
needed a Quantum Computer and I thought that must be even more
in the future than Nanotechnology.

Just a few days ago in my September 17 issue of Science on page
1569 I found an article called " A Potentially Realizable
Quantum Computer" by Seth Lloyd. My understanding of such things
is fuzzy at best and my ignorance is profound. Does anybody know
anything? 
         John K Clark   johnkc@well.sf.ca.us

