Date: Sat, 2 Oct 93 14:13:40 PDT
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: HEALTH: Burning your Health Card

> From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
> On penicillin for colds:  I heard the justification (indirectly from
> a doctor, so take with a pinch of salt) that having a viral cold weakens
> the immune system and makes the sufferer more prone to bacteriological
> invasion - hence the penicillin is a preventative, not a curative.

There may be some basis to this; I don't know.  But I've had my
share of colds, and I think one of them "went bacterial".  This is
not a common or particularly severe complication for people who are
otherwise healthy.  If you develop an allergy to a penicillin, you
may later find yourself in a situation where high-dose IV penicillin
G is the treatment of choice, but simply cannot be used.

Also, you're going to have a real problem with patient compliance
with this kind of prescription.  Antibiotics should be taken in full
dosage for the entire prescribed period, as you want to kill off
everything within the spectrum of the drug, to minimize selection for
resistant strains.  But most people will stop taking the medicine
when their cold goes away, particularly when it wasn't doing them
any good anyway.

