From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: Operationalism (was Meaninglessness)

James Donald's beady eye has spotted that two dictionaries have
slightly different definitions of 'operationalism'.  What a surprise!
Though I'm glad that James has conducted the operation of checking
dictionaries before sounding forth.  We'll make an operationalist of you
yet, my boy!

> Since Price has been clearly unable to concretely identify
> any of the operations required for .... "spin angular momentum"
> or "electron gyromagnetic moment" ....

"concretely identify"?  I suppose you expect me to transmit design
drawings of the original Stern-Gerlach apparatus, do you?  Dream on.

> So identify them!  Don't just wave your hands and claim
> that in principle they could be identified.

That is exactly how I'll leave the situation.  I'll let astronomers
define 'planet', biologists define 'dog' and physicists define 'spin'. 
Okay, I sometimes have pretensions to being an armchair physicist, but
then I've already told James how to define spin, magnetic moment and
angular momentum (via Stern-Gerlach and Noether).  Guess it just slipped
his mind.  Sorry James, I'm on my home territory on physics.  You would
have been better advised to stick with 'dog' - at least there you had
a chance to muddy the waters with husky, coyote and other demiwolves (do
you like Shakespeare, too?).  Or could it be that you actually know
something about biology and wisely decided to shut up?

> Just how do you rotate an elementary particle by two pi? 

I take it, from your silence, that you accept the reality of bras, kets,
state vectors, wavefunctions, quantum fields etc?  That was the point
of this question, or had you forgotten?  As for rotations (of
wavefunctions and quantum fields) the point was that Noether's theorem
would _identify_ (by considering rotations, in this case) the components
of angular momentum _in terms_ of operationally defined quantum fields. 
The ability to actually rotate an elementary particle is irrelevant. 
(Although rotating an electron in some apparatus by +A is identical,
operationally, to rotating the appparatus by -A).  As for his earlier
question of what happens when rotated by two pi, I'm completely unable
to see the point of.  It is well known that the wavefunction is the
same, after a complete rotation, except that the fermionic components
have reversed sign.  Or perhaps he is still thinks that the wavefunction
is "unreal" in some sense?

James Donald concludes his "analysis" of operationalists by saying:

> They appeared to be arguing for the relativity and subjectivity of
> scientific knowledge.

Quite reverse.  By defining the operations or procedures that define
concepts they are able to agree on things.  It's called being objective.

