Subject: BLACKNET:  The truth is thought provoking

At 11:06 PM 10/1/93 -0400, David Friedman wrote:
>My basic instinct is that, in arguing for a proposition (in this case
>unregulated cryptography) one has an obligation to present and deal with
>the strongest arguments against it. That is part of the reason I included
>that section. On the other hand, I am a little worried about giving
>ammunition to the other side. 

This is a real dilemma, since the general public seems very sensitive to
problems with crypto but blind to the advantages (they can see the fleas
but not the dog). Just because we believe the genii is out of the
technological bottle doesn't mean that others won't waste a lot of effort
trying to put him in a legislative bottle.

Seems to me that DDF's instincts are right, that a law review article at
least needs to address the disadvantages, but not dwell on them. I would
try to bring the advantages into stronger focus. Leftwingers like
crypto-democracy, 1st amendment, and crypto-egalitarianism, rightwingers
like finacial privacy and business competitiveness, libertarians like all
of the above. The measures that opponents suggest for controlling crypto
seem ineffective, awkward, expensive, and authoritarian. So why is crypto
such a hard sell?

Dave Burns                      tburns@gmuvax.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)


