Subject: boffins

> "Boffin" is descibed [in OED2] as being of unknown etymology.
> A person engaged in back-room scientific research.

Belatedly I went to Partridge's Dictionary of Slang and Unconventional
English, of which I have both the 1937 edition with 1960 supplement and
the 1989 concise edition.

1960:  "Usually `the boffins', the inventors working for the advancement of
aviation:  R.A.F.  Dating from before the war of 1939-45, it became general
in the Services only in 1944.  A fanciful name of the Lewis Carroll type,
yet with a glance at `baffle' (the bafflings -- the baffling fellows = those
who baffle the enemy) and perhaps at `The Boffin Books'--a delightful series
for children.--2. In the Navy, any officer over 40 years of age: since ca. 1940."

1989:  "It owes something to Dickens, OUR MUTUAL FRIEND, 1864-5, where a
conscientious Mr Boffin is `a very odd-looking old fellow indeed'--and to
William Morris, NEWS FROM NOWHERE, 1891, where another Mr Boffin was a
dustman interested in mathematics.  Ramsey Spencer adds: `So perhaps the
origin is purely literary'.  For `perhaps' I'd substitute `probably'--it's
that sort of word."  Adds a third meaning: "A `swot', a diligent student;
comprehensive schoolchildren's: early 1980s."

