From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: BLACKNET: The truth is thought provoking 

David Friedman says:
> My basic instinct is that, in arguing for a proposition (in this case
> unregulated cryptography) one has an obligation to present and deal with
> the strongest arguments against it. That is part of the reason I included
> that section. On the other hand, I am a little worried about giving
> ammunition to the other side. The public discussion of the disadvantages of
> encryption so far seems to be limited to the argument that it interferes
> with wire taps--which in my view is only the tip of the iceberg. If I
> convince people that widespread cryptography has serious adverse
> consequences, am I doing more harm than good? Or is the other side likely
> to make these arguments in any case? Have they?

I don't think anyone opposed to cryptography has yet made arguments
about the sorts of possibilities we are considering. Personally, I am
reluctant to see them mentioned, for two reasons. The first is that we
have not yet had sufficient chance to study these problems to
formulate coherent responses to them. The second is that I don't like
giving them new ammo -- let them find it on their own.

However, if you decide otherwise, I will note that criminal syndicates
are not generally broken with the aid of communications intercepts.
People generally soliciting for, say, murder services, can likely be
stopped in other ways -- like sting operations. I think law
enforcement can function just fine even with extensive cryptography.
The thing that cryptography is going to make practical in my opinion
is widespread information leakage and cross border economic activity
-- the physical stuff like Murder Inc. is unlikely to work any better
than it does now.

